  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Forrest Mars Jr. helped make the history of chocolate something museum visitors could taste as well as study. He encouraged a Smithsonian symposium on the subject and pressed for serious scholarship. He also urged his company to reproduce historic American chocolate, allowing visitors to experience the spiced flavours of earlier recipes.\n\nHis interest in American history extended to buildings. At Colonial Williamsburg, he funded reconstruction of the coffeehouse, armoury, tin shop and market house, and endowed preservation work. Visits to the restored town had fostered his attachment to it. He later gave funds for an archaeological collections building honouring Nancy and Colin Campbell.",
